---
import Guide from '../../layouts/Guide.astro';
import Toc from '../../components/Toc.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import Kicker from '../../components/Kicker.astro';
import { guideH2, guideP, guideSection } from '../../components/guide-styles';

const h3 = 'margin: 0; font-size: 24px; line-height: 30px; font-weight: 600; letter-spacing: -0.015em';
const cell = 'padding: var(--space-3) var(--space-4)';
const rule = 'border-bottom: 1px solid var(--line)';
const caches = [
  ['Homebrew', '~/Library/Caches/Homebrew', 'brew cleanup --prune=all'],
  ['npm', '~/.npm', 'npm cache clean --force'],
  ['Dart / Flutter', '~/.pub-cache', 'dart pub cache clean'],
];

// One list feeds both the visible FAQ and the FAQPage JSON-LD (answers lose their tags there).
const faq: Array<[string, string]> = [
  ['Is it safe to delete Xcode DerivedData?', 'Yes. DerivedData holds build products, logs and indexes that Xcode recreates automatically. Your next build and code indexing will take longer once while Xcode rebuilds them.'],
  ['Will deleting node_modules break my project?', 'No. As long as package.json and your lockfile are still there, <span class="ic">npm install</span> (or yarn or pnpm) restores the same dependencies.'],
  ['Does docker system prune delete my volumes?', 'Not by default. Volumes are only removed when you add the <span class="ic">--volumes</span> flag.'],
  ['Why does my Mac show developer files as “System Data”?', 'macOS Storage settings don’t break down caches inside your Library or project folders, so most developer build files are counted as System Data. A developer Mac cleaner like CacheDrop names each one and shows its size.'],
  ['What does CacheDrop clean for developers?', 'Xcode DerivedData, old node_modules folders, unused Docker images and build cache, and Homebrew, npm and Dart package caches. You review every item before it’s cleared.'],
];
---
<Guide
  title="Clear Xcode DerivedData, node_modules & Docker Cache on Mac"
  description="Delete Xcode DerivedData, clear old node_modules and Docker caches on your Mac. Manual Terminal steps, plus a free developer Mac cleaner that does it safely."
  path="/clear-mac-developer-caches/"
  crumb="Developer Storage Guide"
  headline="Reclaim 50GB of forgotten code."
  lede="If you write code on a Mac, your biggest space hog isn’t your files. It’s the build artifacts scattered across every old project."
  cta="Download CacheDrop (Free)"
  ctaNote="Developer Mac cleaner · macOS 14+ · Apple Silicon & Intel"
  updated="October 2026"
  dateModified="2026-10-29"
  readTime="6 min read"
  faqTitle="Developer storage FAQ"
  faq={faq}
  other={{ eyebrow: 'Also edit video?', title: 'How to clear Adobe media cache and Final Cut Pro render files', href: '/clear-adobe-media-cache-mac/', link: 'Read the creator guide' }}
  closing={{ title: 'Skip the Terminal.', body: 'CacheDrop finds DerivedData, node_modules and Docker caches in one scan. Free while in beta.' }}
  trademarks="Apple, Mac, macOS and Xcode are trademarks of Apple Inc."
>
<Toc items={[
  ['#derived-data', 'How to delete Xcode DerivedData'],
  ['#node-modules', 'How to clear old node_modules folders on a Mac'],
  ['#docker', 'How to clear Docker and package manager caches'],
  ['#faq', 'Developer storage FAQ'],
]} />

<section style="padding-top: var(--space-16)">
<h2 style={guideH2}>What is eating your Mac’s storage?</h2>
<p style="margin: 18px 0 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 28px; color: var(--ink-body); text-wrap: pretty">When you check macOS storage, developer environments are usually grouped into a massive, unhelpful “System Data” block. CacheDrop specifically targets:</p>
<ul style="margin: var(--space-4) 0 0; padding: 0 0 0 var(--space-5); display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: var(--space-1-5); font-size: 17px; line-height: 28px; color: var(--ink-body)">
<li>Xcode DerivedData: intermediate build output and indexes</li>
<li>Abandoned <span class="ic">node_modules</span> folders in old projects</li>
<li>Docker images and build cache, plus Homebrew, npm and Dart package caches</li>
</ul>
<p style="margin: 18px 0 0; font-size: 17px; line-height: 28px; color: var(--ink-body)">All three are rebuildable: your tools recreate them the next time you build or install. Deleting them frees disk space without touching your source code.</p>
</section>

<section id="derived-data" style={guideSection}>
<h3 style={h3}>1. Xcode DerivedData</h3>
<p style={`${guideP}; text-wrap: pretty`}>Xcode generates massive amounts of intermediate build information. CacheDrop safely clears <span class="ic">~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData</span> without touching your actual code.</p>
<Kicker>Delete DerivedData manually</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>Quit Xcode first, then run this in Terminal. You can also open Xcode › Settings › Locations, click the arrow next to Derived Data, and move the folders to the Trash.</p>
<CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="rm -rf ~/Library/Developer/Xcode/DerivedData/*" />
<Kicker>With CacheDrop</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>CacheDrop shows DerivedData’s size in its Developer caches review, so you can see it and clear it in one click.</p>
</section>

<section id="node-modules" style={guideSection}>
<h3 style={h3}>2. Abandoned node_modules</h3>
<p style={`${guideP}; text-wrap: pretty`}>You probably have dozens of old React or Node projects you haven’t touched in a year. Each <span class="ic">node_modules</span> folder can be 500MB+. CacheDrop finds them all and lets you drop them in one click.</p>
<Kicker>Find and clear node_modules manually</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>List every node_modules folder under a directory with its size. Swap in the folder where you keep projects:</p>
<CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="find ~/Projects -name node_modules -type d -prune -exec du -sh {} +" />
<p style={guideP}>Delete the ones you don’t need with <span class="ic">rm -rf path/to/project/node_modules</span>. If you return to a project, <span class="ic">npm install</span> brings them back.</p>
<Kicker>With CacheDrop</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>CacheDrop scans the folders you choose and lists every node_modules folder with its size, so you can pick the ones to drop.</p>
</section>

<section id="docker" style={guideSection}>
<h3 style={h3}>3. Docker Caches &amp; Package Managers</h3>
<p style={`${guideP}; text-wrap: pretty`}>Clear out unused Docker images, Homebrew caches, <span class="ic">.pub-cache</span>, and <span class="ic">npm</span> leftovers that are quietly eating your SSD.</p>
<Kicker>Clear Docker cache manually</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>This removes stopped containers, unused networks, dangling images and build cache. Add <span class="ic">-a</span> to also remove every image no container is using. Volumes are kept unless you add <span class="ic">--volumes</span>.</p>
<CodeBlock label="Terminal" code="docker system prune" />
<Kicker>Package manager caches</Kicker>
<div style="overflow-x: auto; border: 1px solid var(--line); border-radius: var(--radius-lg)">
<table style="width: 100%; min-width: 560px; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: 14px; line-height: 20px; text-align: left">
<thead>
<tr style="background: var(--surface-elevated)">
{['Tool', 'Cache location', 'Clear it with'].map((h) => <th scope="col" style={`${cell}; font-weight: 600; ${rule}`}>{h}</th>)}
</tr>
</thead>
<tbody>
{caches.map(([tool, where, how], i) => {
  const last = i === caches.length - 1;
  const td = `${cell}; font-size: 13px; color: var(--ink-body)${last ? '' : `; ${rule}`}`;
  return (
<tr>
<th scope="row" style={`${cell}; font-weight: 500${last ? '' : `; ${rule}`}`}>{tool}</th>
<td class="mono" style={td}>{where}</td>
<td class="mono" style={td}>{how}</td>
</tr>
  );
})}
</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<Kicker>With CacheDrop</Kicker>
<p style={guideP}>CacheDrop groups these caches under Developer caches and build files, shows the total, and clears them together. No commands to remember.</p>
</section>
</Guide>

<style is:global>
.ic{white-space:nowrap}
</style>
